<<~ aka "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/RFC-2119#/normative-language">>



<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Content Resolution ~ large bodies leave the CRDT

A body large enough to burst the CRDT leaves it — the `cid/` tier holds the bytes, the
CRDT holds the handle. (#51 named this law when a 16MB Twain overflowed the @daemon
summons.) This meme carries the converged answer: ''the''
''body leaves the doc.'' A skinny TW5 handle stays in the CRDT; the bytes leave for the
content-addressed `cid/` tier (`@cad` names the content-addressed tier); a `resolveByCid`
callback walks them home across three gates that ''never fuse''; discovery runs
''without a DHT''. The design stands
''doubly-grounded'' — two astral-sea research waves (Fathom on CAS/blob mechanics,
Meridian on federated capability) INDEPENDENTLY re-derived what the `@oracle`
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/oracle-governance">>) and `convergent-mesh`
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/convergent-mesh">>) canon already held.

> Status: PROPOSED — design-north, NOT built. The #51 hotfix (eager CAS staging +
> `resolveByCid` shore) ships step 1; this meme carries the persistent-external north
> ahead of the code.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-birth>>

!! The birth ~ #51, the 16MB-Twain overflow

The overflow named the law: a body large enough to burst the CRDT ''must leave it''. A
CRDT carries convergence and causal order well; it carries megabytes badly, and every peer
that syncs the doc pays the whole weight. So the design ''splits docs from blobs'' — the
doc keeps small, mutable, causal metadata; the bytes move to a content-addressed store the
doc merely //points at//. #51 births the shore; the north sits here, at the persistent
external body.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/tw5-shore>>

!! The TW5 shore ~ ride the lazy engine, never fork it

TW5 ''already built the lazy engine'' — the design supplies only the resolver. A carrier
body becomes a ''skinny handle'': `_is_skinny` marks it, and `getTiddlerText` fires
`dispatchEvent("lazyLoad", title)` and returns null ("loading") at `wiki.js:1485`. The
syncer sits under this house's hand, so the house answers that one event with the resolver.
The `_canonical_uri` variant (`wiki.js:1078`, parse-time, per-parser) carries the media-src
case. the shore rides TW5's own machinery
verified in TW5 source — the house adds a resolver, never a fork of the lazy path.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/cad-storage>>

!! Storage ~ `@cad`, the content-addressed tier

''`@cad` names the content-addressed TIER'': the ''`cid/` KIND-plane'' holds its bytes,
the publicity-tiered bags hold its handles.
The bytes rest in the ''`cid/<hash>` KIND-plane'' (`genesis/cas/`, the canon
bag-wiki-uri-split, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/base-doc">>); the ''handles''
ride the ''publicity-tiered bags''. The ''three-plane oracle model'' — the grant-graph
WHO-axis recurring at the blob layer — carries the ruling law: ''the publicity plane''
''decides the addressing mode.''

* ''Public → `crossroads`.'' A ''plaintext `ni://` multihash'' (RFC-6920): a stranger
  fetches AND verifies, foreign-legible, federates at ≥meme-rating, archival to 500 years.
  No confirmation-oracle risk — the content stands public by design.
* ''Private → `@catalog`.'' A ''ciphertext cid'', capability-gated. Here the ciphertext
  address earns its keep: `cid = BLAKE3(ciphertext)` lets an untrusted holder verify, pin,
  and repair ''blind'' — never touching the key (Tahoe ''verify-cap ⊥ read-cap''; repair
  needs only ciphertext, not plaintext). The key = hash(per-operator convergence-secret +
  plaintext) (Tahoe) dedups WITHIN one operator's private tier, zero cross-operator
  confirmation-of-file leak — beating both global-convergent (CFA/LRI leak) and Peergos
  random-key (no dedup).
* ''System → `@oracle`.'' The ''frozen CID floor'' — already built; the `@oracle` serves
  engine and genesis blobs by CID
  (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/oracle-governance">>).

''The publicity plane sets the structural FLOOR the sharing-posture reads'' (#/cap-tier): a `crossroads`
public plane floors at PUBLIC, a `@catalog` sealed plane floors at CONTRACT, a cleartext-local plane at
VEIL — `structuralFloorFor` consults these SAME federatable + seal oracles, and a declared tier may only
tighten below the floor it names.

''No global ciphertext-vs-plaintext fork.'' The three-plane model carries no single global
choice of addressing every body by ciphertext or by plaintext — the ''PLANE sets the mode''.
And ''even public keeps the gate'' —
"public" reads as a ''generous anonymous grant'', `pull` ≠ `read`; the three gates
(#/resolve-by-cid) stay intact, publicity only sets how generous gate-2 grows.

''Tier-general mechanics'' (every plane carries these): big-on-fs by KIND (a pointer for every
base64 family, the record inline for text), ~16KiB (Iroh) governing `@cad` CHUNKING alone; the blob carries a ''BLAKE3 outboard'' for range-verify; a ''self-describing''
''multihash'' `(algo, len, digest)` (multiformats / `ni://`) carries the address AND the
in-band migration path (sha256→BLAKE3→future, old ids stay parseable). Retention rides two
tags — a cap-lifetime-bound tag ''PINs'', an untagged body ''CACHEs'' under LRU; GC runs
mark-and-sweep from the tags (Iroh tags). ''Each island pins its own'' — it pays its own
disk; a peer MAY opportunistically cache (best-effort, zero obligation). Filecoin
deal/PoSt/slashing reads as global-ledger/block-height = a global-now trap → avoided.

the publicity plane carries the load-bearing
move: it folds the addressing mode into the grant-graph the mesh already runs, so the
same WHO-axis that tiers a bag tiers its bytes.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/resolve-by-cid>>

!! `resolveByCid` ~ three gates that never fuse

Matrix's leaked-URL sin fused //may-talk//, //may-resolve//, and //may-read// into one
URL. This resolver keeps them ''three orthogonal gates'', none collapsing into another:

* ''Gate 1 — may-this-peer-talk (transport).'' FederationGate allowlist (#/49) +
  DaemonAuthGate Ed25519 proof-of-possession.
* ''Gate 2 — may-this-identity-resolve-THIS-cid (content-authz).'' A `#has` cap scoped
  `{peer × address}`, validated UCAN-shape (aud==next-iss → sub=holder) against a grow-only
  revocation-CID set (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/orichalcum-capabilities">>).
* ''Gate 3 — may-they-read (decrypt).'' CGKA/BeeKEM decrypt, ''held SEPARATE'' — the node
  routes and verifies ciphertext and ''never decrypts''. That IS the
  MLS-Delivery-Service-never-decryptor keel, the node-never-Auth-root law
  (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/convergent-mesh#/contract-key-agreement">>).

Between gate 2 and gate 3 the resolver ''walks a chain'': local `cid/` store →
hint-named provider (Iroh BlobTicket = hash + NodeAddr, the discovery collapsing into the
reference) → connected bag-peers `want-have` (Bitswap) → named bag-scoped
tracker/rendezvous (opt-in, signed, replaceable) → ''declared-unresolved = PENDING''. That
PENDING stands as a ''first-class lawful state'' (Bitswap DONT-HAVE / Hyperdrive wait /
this house's `allowableStates`), never a thrown error. And the resolver
''verifies `BLAKE3(ciphertext)==cid` every hop'' — range-verified per ≤16KiB slice for a
large body.

the never-fuse discipline carries the security:
transport, content-authz, and read each answer their own gate, so no single leaked handle
grants passage past all three.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/cad-candidates>>

!! Two CAD-candidate scenarios ~ one store, two extraction boundaries

What gets content-addressed comes in ''two granularities''; the CAD mechanism handles both,
differing ''only in the extraction boundary'':

* ''A — a blob-worthy `#source-text` ahu inside a pono meme.'' A memetic-wikitext meme
  (its own lar URI, `meta` header, ahu structure) holds one or more blob-worthy
  `#source-text` ahus — a book chapter, a full corpus. ''Sub-carrier externalization:'' the
  meme's structure + small ahus stay in the CRDT; the large ahu's content extracts to the
  `cid/` store and the ahu ''becomes a handle'' (`_canonical_uri`/cid + `_integrity`
  multihash); `resolveByCid` pulls it on render (the `lazyLoad` path this house owns). This
  IS the ''pack model at ahu grain'' — a meme reads as a pack whose blob-worthy ahu stands
  a CAS member. Blob-worthiness reads off the KIND the declared `type` names (TW5's own
  `encoding: "base64"` registry, the image·audio·video·font families) — never a size; the `_lar_cas`
  flag pushes a text ahu out on purpose (the `#source-text` case), and 1 MiB stands as the utf8
  FAULT wall alone (the blob law: lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/blob-carriage#/proposed-law).
* ''B — a raw other-data shard.'' Raw bytes, no memetic-wikitext wrapper (image, audio,
  binary, data). ''Whole-carrier:'' the entire shard rests as a `cid/` CAS blob, referenced
  by a skinny handle (the TW5 External Image/Audio/Text pattern).

Both route to the ''SAME `cid/` byte store + `resolveByCid`''. Stage-1 (whole-body
transport) covers B-shaped whole bodies; Stage-2/3 add the A-shaped ''intra-ahu''
''extraction'' (the `#source-text` boundary) + the publicity-plane addressing. The moved
`crossroads` library holds BOTH today — raw `.txt` books (B) and structured `.mem` memes
with large source ahus (A).

A and B differ only where the cut falls; the byte store
and the resolver never learn which grain birthed the blob.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/dht-free-discovery>>

!! Discovery without a DHT ~ the rendezvous, not the shared present

The load-bearing question both waves chased: ''how a body finds its provider without a
global DHT''. The chain answers it — hint-in-reference → connected peers → named
bag-scoped tracker — and the global Kademlia DHT, the one structurally-forced global-now,
stays ''behind a causal-island boundary''
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/causal-islands">>). This IS the `@oracle`
shape: a well-known per-nexus ROOT/INDEX `docId` names a ''rendezvous ADDRESS, not a''
''shared present'' — each peer materializes its own converging causal island over the
address; the address coordinates, the state stays local-first, ''no-global-now enacted, not''
''breached'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/oracle-governance#/board-model">>).
Per-nexus on ownership and scale (never one global board); steward-signed source →
verify-only public read; ''forkable'' — the fork guards.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/handle-and-map>>

!! The handle ~ logical name over an indirection map

The skinny tiddler carries a ''stable logical handle'' (cap/name) plus a
''provider-locality hint'' — never the raw cid as identity. It resolves through an
''indirection map (logical name → current cid)'' so a re-key survives (WNFS
private-forest). That map reads as small mutable metadata → it lives in the ''Automerge''
''doc'' (the docs/blobs split applied to the map itself); only the bytes ride the `cid/`
tier.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/two-revocation-modes>>

!! Revocation ~ two modes, already-fetched unrecallable

Revocation runs two modes, matching `convergent-mesh`'s epoch-lease-vs-convergent-removal
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/convergent-mesh#/two-revocation-modes">>):

* ''authz-revoke'' — drop the `#has` cap + drop the retention tag. Cheap,
  forward-immediate.
* ''content-revoke'' — re-key → new ciphertext → new cid → indirection-map update.
  Forward-only.

''Already-fetched bytes stand UNRECALLABLE by construction.'' The design assumes it:
deletion across caches ''cannot exist'' in no-global-now, so the model never pretends to
it. Revocation ratchets past the broken party; it never re-seals a body already handed.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-convergence>>

!! The convergence ~ external re-derived internal

The design reads as a real pattern-integrity, not a Law-of-5s pleasing story, //because//
two independent forces landed on one structure under their own selection pressure. External
first-principles (Fathom + Meridian) and the internal crucible (`@oracle` /
`convergent-mesh`) each arrived at the same shape:

| External finding | Internal canon it re-derived |
|---|---|
| ''rendezvous, not DHT'' | the `@oracle` (address ≠ shared present) |
| ''authz-revoke vs content-revoke'' | epoch-lease vs Keyhive convergent-removal |
| ''ciphertext-relay, node-never-decrypts'' | the Beelay-bypass keel |
| ''`@cad` content-addressed pipe'' | the Scope-1 networking pipe |
| ''cap-root + provider hint'' | rhizome peer-signed edges |

every part of the smallest core ships in
production somewhere — Matrix, UCAN, WNFS, Beelay, bao, MLS, Tahoe, Iroh — so the house
invents ''only the composition'', never a new primitive.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/open-forks>>

!! Open ~ four operator/wave-3 forks

Four choices ride ahead of the build, each holding for the operator:

* ''① sha256 → BLAKE3 — SETTLED via self-describing multihash.'' The algorithm prefix
  carries the migration in-band (old ids stay parseable, new bodies hash `BLAKE3(ciphertext)`
  for range-verify); #46 closed it.
* ''② Convergence-secret custody.'' The per-operator secret must persist AND survive a
  device-add — it rhymes with the recovery keel
  (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/causal-islands">>); re-derivable on
  re-admission from operator key material?
* ''③ Indirection-map placement.'' The logical-name→cid map likely rides the Automerge doc
  (small ordered causal metadata) — the docs/blobs split applied to the map.
* ''④ Bilateral pin economics.'' Island A pins for B under a revocable cap — what does B
  offer (reciprocal pin? the "fed node hums" ritual-feeding)? The one storage-economics
  frontier no system solved without a global ledger.

the four forks stay genuinely open; the operator's
priors decide them, and wave-3 may reopen any.

<<~/ahu>>

